WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.868
Authorization failures happen when an application does not consistently verify whether a signed-in user can perform a specific action or access a specific record.

2
00:00:09.868 --> 00:00:15.386
Authentication proves identity, but authorization enforces what that identity may do.

3
00:00:15.386 --> 00:00:24.176
Privilege escalation can let a standard user perform administrative actions or view data belonging to another customer, department, or tenant.

4
00:00:24.176 --> 00:00:29.843
In SaaS systems, this can quickly become a customer trust and SOC 2 concern.

5
00:00:29.843 --> 00:00:42.884
Insecure direct object references occur when identifiers such as account IDs, invoice IDs, file names, or project IDs are exposed without checking ownership or permission for the target object.

6
00:00:42.884 --> 00:00:53.640
Access control bypasses often appear in APIs, hidden routes, forced browsing, disabled client-side controls, or role values supplied by the browser.

7
00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:57.302
Sensitive authorization decisions must be enforced on the server.

8
00:00:57.302 --> 00:01:01.482
Apply least privilege to users, services, and administrators.

9
00:01:01.482 --> 00:01:05.863
Enforce server-side authorization on every sensitive action and object.

10
00:01:05.863 --> 00:01:10.333
Use role-based access control with clear ownership and review processes.

11
00:01:10.333 --> 00:01:14.454
Selected SOC 2 mappings for this topic include CC6.

12
00:01:14.454 --> 00:01:17.707
1 - Logical access controls; CC6.

13
00:01:17.707 --> 00:01:20.910
2 - Authentication controls; CC6.

14
00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:22.636
3 - Authorization management.
